# The NanoPC-T6 board glue (nanopc_t6.c over driver/rust's ip::dw_wdt,
# soc::rk3588 and aarch64): every image built for the board has it, so no
# image can run without its bounded watchdog.

config YAKOGL_NANOPC_T6
	def_bool BOARD_NANOPC_T6
	select YAKOGL_DRV_DISPLAY_RUST
	help
	  The early banner (raw T6:A/A1/A2/A3 lines on uart2 before the
	  console, T6:X for an exception taken to EL2) and HCR_EL2 at entry;
	  WDT0 (the DesignWare APB watchdog at 0xfeaf0000) armed at
	  PRE_KERNEL_1 and fed from a timer for a bounded time; the GICv3 CPU
	  interface put back into its reset EOI mode at EARLY; and
	  t6_reset_to_linux() (nanopc_t6.h), the end of a run: PSCI
	  SYSTEM_RESET, with WDT0 behind it. YAKOGL_DRV_DISPLAY_RUST is the switch
	  that builds the yakogl-drivers crate (driver/zephyr/CMakeLists.txt,
	  feature rk3588).

if YAKOGL_NANOPC_T6

config YAKOGL_NANOPC_T6_VHE_EXIT
	bool "Write HCR_EL2 := RW at entry (leave Linux's VHE state)"
	default y
	help
	  Armbian's Linux runs at EL2 with VHE, and its kexec enters the
	  image with HCR_EL2.E2H (and FMO) still set. Zephyr's
	  z_arm64_el2_init keeps both, writes CNTHCTL_EL2 in the E2H=0
	  layout, and EL1's first physical-timer write in z_arm64_el1_init
	  then traps to EL2, where nothing of the image's is: the first jump
	  on 2026-10-19 printed nothing (driver/rust/src/aarch64.rs). With
	  this, the image's first code writes HCR_EL2 = RW only, as Linux's
	  own entry writes a whole HCR_EL2. n keeps Linux's value: the A/B
	  arm that shows the failure (T6:A with E2H set, then T6:X).

config YAKOGL_NANOPC_T6_WDT_TOP
	int "WDT0 period: TOP (period = 2^(16 + TOP) / 24 MHz)"
	default 13
	range 11 15
	help
	  11: 5.6 s, 12: 11.2 s, 13: 22.4 s, 14: 44.7 s, 15: 89.5 s. The
	  board resets at most one period after the feeding stops. Below 11
	  a missed tick of the 1 s feeder would already reset the board.

config YAKOGL_NANOPC_T6_LIFETIME_S
	int "Seconds of uptime the watchdog is fed at most"
	default 60
	range 10 3600
	help
	  After this nothing feeds WDT0 and the board resets within one
	  period, also when the image hangs or faults. The run must end
	  before it (t6_reset_to_linux()).

config YAKOGL_NANOPC_T6_ALIVE_S
	int "Seconds between two t6_watchdog_feed() calls at most"
	default 5
	range 1 600
	help
	  Once the sample has called t6_watchdog_feed(), a longer gap stops
	  the feeding, so a stuck sample is reset long before the lifetime.

config YAKOGL_NANOPC_T6_WDT_PROOF
	bool "Proof: arm WDT0 and never feed it"
	help
	  The measurement of the reset path itself: WDT0 is armed and not
	  fed, and t6_reset_to_linux() waits for it instead of calling PSCI.
	  A software backstop (PSCI SYSTEM_RESET) fires 10 s after one period
	  and prints "t6: proof FAILED" first.

endif # YAKOGL_NANOPC_T6
